# Derived run reports — regenerated by their own script on every --write pass.
# Never a source of truth: read the committed assets/data.js instead.
engine/rollforward_ledger.json
engine/technicals_report.json

# Bytecode. THE SEPARATE DECISION THIS NOTE LEFT OPEN WAS MADE 07-09-2026 AND SIX
# .pyc FILES WERE UNTRACKED. The note was right that .gitignore does not untrack
# what is already tracked, and right to leave the choice rather than take it in
# passing — what forced it is that [R-ENF-01 EXTENDED 07-09-2026]'s tree-unmodified
# gate asks whether running the checks CHANGED the tree, and a .pyc header embeds
# the source's mtime, which on a fresh checkout is the checkout time. So every CI
# run recompiled three of them and the gate refused, correctly: THE TRACKED TREE
# DID CHANGE. Leaving them tracked meant a permanently red check, which [R-ENF-02]
# forbids, and teaching the gate to ignore .pyc would have suppressed a true
# report. Nothing reads them — every reference to __pycache__ in this repository
# is an EXCLUSION in a sandbox or a walker — and all six sources are present, so
# they are pure caches. They were last re-committed by the UNATTENDED PIPELINE,
# whose `git add -A` swept them back in because a tracked file ignores .gitignore;
# untracking stops that at the source. The rules below now bind on them.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# scripts/check_ta_chart_overlay.js — the MANDATORY overlay gate — needs playwright,
# and there is no package.json, so running it means `npm install --no-save playwright`
# in the repo root. That leaves ~100MB of untracked noise in `git status` on every
# pass that honours the gate. The browser binary itself is pre-provisioned at
# /opt/pw-browsers/chromium (the script hardcodes that executablePath), so only the
# node package is ever fetched.
node_modules/

# Phase C point-in-time fair-value cache (derived from git history; self-invalidating)
engine/.pit_fair_values_*.json
engine/.pit_tmp_data.js

# Local virtualenv created to run the engine (numpy/pandas/scipy/openpyxl/python-docx).
# Build tooling, never a source of truth.
.venv/
# AIRARABIA study source documents (62MB of primary PDFs from airarabia.com IR +
# their local OCR text). Provenance lives in the study's bibliography document;
# the PDFs themselves are retrievable from the URLs recorded there.
engine/airarabia_study/sources/
# BOROUGE primary source PDFs — 78 MB of company filings. Excluded deliberately, NOT
# because they are unimportant but because the provenance chain does not depend on
# storing them: engine/borouge_study/source_access.json carries the public URL, the byte
# count and the SHA-256 of all 21, and verify_sources.py re-downloads and re-hashes every
# one. A hash plus a public URL proves more than a committed binary does. The extracted
# .txt the build actually reads IS committed. See engine/borouge_study/src/README.md.
engine/borouge_study/src/*.pdf

# Day-0 refetch: primary documents re-fetched and sha256-verified against the walk-forward registers stay on disk for the session; the verified url+sha record is engine/method_reassessment/refetch_log.json. The base-year and latest-interim statements each study stands on are committed under the study directories explicitly.
engine/*_walkforward/filings/

# The framework build board is GENERATED and deliberately NOT committed: GitHub Pages
# serves this whole tree, so an .html file here is served on the live site, and this
# page carries internal vocabulary — gates, ratchets, held studies. Committed: the
# generator (build_board.py) and its state (board_state.json). Rendered: to a scratch
# path, and published from there as an artefact.
engine/method_reassessment/board.html

# session tokens and fetch scratch — never committed, the repository is public
**/.cookies
**/.rc.html
**/text/.ocrtmp-*.png

# OCR page scratch and bytecode — regenerable, never committed
**/text/.pg-*.png
**/.pg-*.png
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

# Step 2A sweep working output: OCR page renders and pdftotext extractions.
# DERIVED, and regenerable from the filings beside them, which ARE committed because
# SIGCM wants the source reachable. A rendered page is not a source — it is a reading
# of one, and the sweep register records which route produced each figure. HRHO alone
# is 15MB of renders and 4.5MB of text against 46MB of filings that carry the meaning.
engine/*_study_pending/ocr/
engine/*_study_pending/txt/
engine/*_study/ocr/
engine/*_study/txt/
# The same files also land UNDER filings/ and beside them, because each sweep agent
# picks its own layout. The rule is about what the file IS, not where it sits: a
# rendered page and a pdftotext dump are readings of a source, regenerable from the PDF
# committed next to them, and the sweep register records which route produced each
# figure. FWRY put its renders under filings/ocr/ and the first pattern missed them.
engine/*_study_pending/filings/ocr/
engine/*_study_pending/filings/pages/
engine/*_study_pending/filings/*_OCR.txt
engine/*_walkforward/ocr/
engine/*_walkforward/txt/
# Same class under a third name. The ADIB run called its extraction directory pages/,
# and it holds both the per-page text dumps and a _tmp.png the renderer overwrites once
# per page -- 1.9 MB of scratch that would enter history on every page of every filing.
# The RULE is the one already written above for ocr/ and txt/: the extraction OUTPUT is
# an intermediate, the extraction SCRIPT and the filings are the artefacts, and the
# script records the route each figure came by [R-FCAL-01 §1] so the output is
# reproducible from what is committed. Named here rather than left to whoever notices.
engine/*_walkforward/pages/
# A LOG A RUNNING PROCESS IS STILL WRITING. ocr.log was swept into a commit by a
# `git add -A` while the ADIB extraction was mid-flight, so every subsequent branch
# operation hit "Please commit your changes or stash them" against a file that changes
# by itself. A log is the RECORD OF a run, not an artefact of it: the route each figure
# came by is recorded in the run's own JSON, which is what [R-FCAL-01 §1] asks for.
engine/*_walkforward/*.log
# Raw probe output — web-archive CDX index dumps and the like. A query RESULT is not a
# source document: what SIGCM wants reachable is the filing, which lands in filings/ or
# src/ and IS committed, and what the protocol wants logged is the access attempt with its
# real outcome, which lands in the sweep register. These are the scratch behind both, and
# they are not even a clean artefact set — two of HELI's six do not parse as JSON at all,
# being failed probes saved under a .json name, which is exactly what scratch looks like.
engine/*_study_pending/raw/
engine/*_study/raw/
# OCDI's agent nests its derived output one level deeper again — src/ocr/ and src/txt.
# Fifth layout tonight. Same rule, same reason: a render and an extraction are readings
# of a source, and the source itself (src/pdf/) is committed.
engine/*_study_pending/src/ocr/
engine/*_study_pending/src/txt/
engine/*_study_pending/src/txt_*.txt

# Transient gate scratch that lands in the repository ROOT. No committed script writes
# these — they are shell redirections from gate runs in a session, exit-code/command pairs
# and captured output. They have been deleted by hand three times tonight, which is the
# signal to stop deleting and start ignoring. The repository root is not a scratch
# directory; anything that belongs is named and committed.
/.o.txt
/.rerun.txt
/.rerun_res.txt

# CAPTURED THIRD-PARTY SOURCE PAGES ARE EVIDENCE, NOT PAGES WE PUBLISH.
# GitHub Pages serves the whole repository (there is a .nojekyll), so any .html
# committed anywhere is a page a reader can land on — check_page_integrity.py says
# so in its own docstring, and it was right to fire. It caught the favicon; the
# larger fact it exposed is that three captured sodic.com pages were being SERVED
# FROM OUR DOMAIN, 1.8MB of somebody else's markup, linked from nothing.
#
# The remedy is not to brand a supplier's page with our favicon — that would be
# editing captured evidence to satisfy a check. It is to stop publishing it. The
# provenance that matters is the URL, the access date and the finding, all of which
# live in the sweep register; the byte copy was only ever convenience, and the same
# reasoning already excludes ocr/ and txt/ under these directories.
# ANCHORED AT engine/, BECAUSE A PATTERN CONTAINING A SLASH IS ANCHORED AT THE
# .gitignore's OWN DIRECTORY. Written first as `*_study_pending/src/*.html`, which
# matches only a directory of that name at the REPOSITORY ROOT — these live under
# engine/, so it matched nothing, the files were re-added by the next `git add -A
# engine`, and check_page_integrity went red again on a fix that had already landed.
engine/*_study_pending/src/*.html
engine/*_study_pending/src/**/*.html

# THE CI VERDICT AND THE PROGRESS SNAPSHOT ARE ATTESTATIONS ABOUT A COMMIT, so
# committing either guarantees it is stale: the commit that ADDS the record is not the
# commit the record describes, and [R-ENF-06] would correctly call it out on arrival.
# Both regenerate in seconds — record_ci_verdict.py reads the checks API for HEAD, and
# progress.py --json rebuilds its own snapshot — so neither is a fact anyone needs
# stored. A file that states something which moves must not be the thing that remembers
# it.
engine/build_depth_audit/ci_gate_run.json
engine/method_reassessment/progress.json
